  "answer": "Ohm’s law states that the potential difference (V) across a conductor is directly proportional to the current (I) passing through it, provided the temperature and other physical conditions remain constant. Mathematically, it is expressed as V = IR, where R is the resistance of the conductor. To verify Ohm’s law, an experiment can be conducted as follows: Set up a circuit with a battery, a resistor, an ammeter, and a voltmeter. Connect the ammeter in series with the resistor and the voltmeter in parallel across the resistor. Adjust the battery voltage by using multiple cells in series and record the corresponding current and voltage readings. Calculate the ratio V/I for each set of values. If the ratio remains constant, it confirms Ohm’s law. A graph of V versus I should be a straight line passing through the origin, indicating a linear relationship.",
